1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,810
Loopback Transformer:

2
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:05,510
Towards a Unified View of
Residual Looped Transformers.

3
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:09,550
Linear attention is a memory over tokens:

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:13,290
each token writes its key
and value into a state,

5
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:14,940
the state is passed along,

6
00:00:14,940 --> 00:00:17,250
and later tokens read from it.

7
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:20,590
The residual stream does
the same over depth.

8
00:00:21,100 --> 00:00:23,890
Each block writes its
update into the stream.

9
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:25,860
The stream carries it forward,

10
00:00:25,860 --> 00:00:28,250
and later blocks read from it.

11
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:31,760
Unrolled, every read is a
weighted sum of what was written;

12
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:35,650
plain residual addition
fixes every weight at one.

13
00:00:37,310 --> 00:00:38,090
In this view,

14
00:00:38,090 --> 00:00:40,340
every residual rule sets three things:

15
00:00:40,390 --> 00:00:41,380
the input's strength,

16
00:00:41,610 --> 00:00:42,840
the total weight of the updates,

17
00:00:42,850 --> 00:00:44,260
and how that weight is shared.

18
00:00:44,850 --> 00:00:47,550
Residual addition gives
every update a weight of one,

19
00:00:47,550 --> 00:00:49,400
so the total grows with every pass.

20
00:00:50,170 --> 00:00:52,980
Hyper-Connections give
each stream one weight,

21
00:00:53,090 --> 00:00:56,280
so the input and the
updates in it move together.

22
00:00:56,890 --> 00:01:00,500
Attention Residuals weigh the input
and the updates with one softmax,

23
00:01:00,610 --> 00:01:02,260
so they compete for a fixed total.

24
00:01:03,930 --> 00:01:05,920
Loopback sets the three separately.

25
00:01:06,530 --> 00:01:08,920
The input keeps weight
one on its own path,

26
00:01:09,150 --> 00:01:10,620
a decay bounds the total,

27
00:01:10,970 --> 00:01:13,560
and attention shares it
across the stored updates.

28
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:19,310
Here are our training
curves at all five scales.

29
00:01:20,020 --> 00:01:24,030
Loopback's loss is lower from early
in training all the way to the end.

30
00:01:26,230 --> 00:01:29,220
From 191M to 3B parameters,

31
00:01:29,630 --> 00:01:32,220
Loopback gets lower loss than the standard

32
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:34,960
Transformer with 40–75% of its parameters.

33
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:38,790
Against seven other looped designs,

34
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:41,313
it has the lowest loss and the highest

35
00:01:41,313 --> 00:01:44,150
benchmark average at all
three sizes we tested.

36
00:01:46,330 --> 00:01:50,000
We also held the parameter count
fixed and traded width for depth.

37
00:01:50,310 --> 00:01:52,120
As the model gets deeper and narrower,

38
00:01:52,450 --> 00:01:54,400
the standard Transformer loses ground,

39
00:01:54,590 --> 00:01:56,900
while Loopback's margin
grows at both scales.

40
00:01:58,600 --> 00:01:59,570
That's Loopback.

41
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:00,550
Fewer parameters,

42
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:01,450
lower loss.

43
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:03,850
The paper and code are
on our project page.

